{
  "id": "math-pythagoras-myp",
  "subjectId": "math",
  "title": "Pythagoras' Theorem",
  "description": "Using a² + b² = c² to find unknown sides in right-angled triangles, Pythagorean triples, and 2D and 3D applications.",
  "ibLevel": "MYP",
  "notes": [
    {
      "id": "math-pythagoras-myp-pyt-n1",
      "heading": "The Theorem and Finding the Hypotenuse",
      "body": "In any right-angled triangle: a² + b² = c², where c is the hypotenuse (side opposite the right angle, always longest). To find the hypotenuse: c = √(a²+b²). Example: legs 3 and 4 → c = √(9+16) = 5."
    },
    {
      "id": "math-pythagoras-myp-pyt-n2",
      "heading": "Finding a Shorter Side and Pythagorean Triples",
      "body": "To find a shorter side: a = √(c²−b²). Example: hypotenuse 13, leg 5 → a = √(169−25) = 12. Pythagorean triples: integer sets satisfying the theorem — 3-4-5, 5-12-13, 8-15-17, 7-24-25. Multiples also work (6-8-10 = 2× 3-4-5)."
    },
    {
      "id": "math-pythagoras-myp-pyt-n3",
      "heading": "Testing for Right Angles and Distance in 2D",
      "body": "To test if triangle is right-angled: check if a²+b²=c² (where c is the longest). If yes → right-angled; if a²+b²>c² → acute; if a²+b²<c² → obtuse. Distance between (x₁,y₁) and (x₂,y₂): d = √((x₂−x₁)² + (y₂−y₁)²) — a direct Pythagoras application."
    },
    {
      "id": "math-pythagoras-myp-pyt-n4",
      "heading": "3D Applications",
      "body": "Space diagonal of cuboid (l×w×h): first find base diagonal d₁=√(l²+w²), then space diagonal d=√(d₁²+h²) = √(l²+w²+h²). Example: 3×4×12: base=5, space=√(25+144)=13."
    }
  ],
  "flashcards": [
